From 9ee1271eea467ba906523ed68f665c31ece01c20 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: adbtrr_work5_kasan_flat_only <adbtrr_work5_kasan_flat_only@local>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 17:12:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] mm: report an allocation failure over the low level console

mm_manage_alloc_fail() takes a critical section and then dumps the heap.
With CONFIG_MM_ASSERT_ON_FAIL off, mfdbg() resolves to dbg(), which is
syslog(), which writes through the serial driver. When the transmit
buffer fills, uart_putxmitchar() enables the transmit interrupt and then
waits on dev->xmitsem for it. Interrupts are masked, so uart_datasent()
never runs and nothing ever posts that semaphore. The allocating thread
never returns from its malloc, the failure is never reported, and the
board stops with nothing on the console but whatever fitted in the
transmit buffer before it filled.

There is no PANIC() in that configuration either, so there is no assert,
no stack dump and no reboot reason. The symptom is a silent hang inside
an allocator call, which is a long way from the cause.

sem_wait() does have an abort mode escape, but it only applies in
interrupt context, and this runs in a task with interrupts merely
masked, so nothing rescues it.

On a flat rtl8721csm image this is reached as soon as the heap runs out.
A KASan build gets there sooner than most: the shadow map is carved from
the tail of every region, which costs 14,604 bytes of the 466,944 byte
SRAM region and 98,316 of the 3,145,728 byte PSRAM region, about 110 KB
of heap in total, on top of a larger image. A heap that was already
close to its limit then fails, typically at the task_tcb_s allocation in
thread_create(), and the board hangs there instead of saying what
happened.

mfdbg() already picks the low level polled path when abort_mode is set,
and this dump always runs with interrupts disabled, so claim abort mode
for its duration whatever the configuration and give it back if we are
going to return. Builds with CONFIG_MM_ASSERT_ON_FAIL set it already and
panic immediately afterwards, so their behaviour is unchanged.

The low level path is lowsyslog() through lib_lowoutstream() to
up_lowputc(), which is polled and touches neither the serial driver nor
a semaphore, so it works with interrupts off. CONFIG_ARCH_LOWPUTC is set
on this target, so it is the genuine article rather than an alias for
syslog().

Both halves of the conditional compile clean under -Wall -Wextra
-Werror.
---
 os/mm/mm_heap/mm_manage_allocfail.c | 30 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 28 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/os/mm/mm_heap/mm_manage_allocfail.c b/os/mm/mm_heap/mm_manage_allocfail.c
index deccc093..d4133ed2 100644
--- a/os/mm/mm_heap/mm_manage_allocfail.c
+++ b/os/mm/mm_heap/mm_manage_allocfail.c
@@ -108,10 +108,28 @@ void mm_manage_alloc_fail_dump(struct mm_heap_s *heap, int startidx, int endidx,
 	up_cpu_pause_all();
 #endif
 	extern bool abort_mode;
-#ifdef CONFIG_MM_ASSERT_ON_FAIL
-	abort_mode = true;
+#ifndef CONFIG_MM_ASSERT_ON_FAIL
+	bool saved_abort_mode = abort_mode;
 #endif
 
+	/* This runs with interrupts disabled, because mm_manage_alloc_fail()
+	 * takes a critical section around it, so the report has to leave over
+	 * the low level polled console.
+	 *
+	 * mfdbg() picks that path only when abort_mode is set or when it is
+	 * called from an interrupt handler. Neither holds here once
+	 * CONFIG_MM_ASSERT_ON_FAIL is off: mfdbg() then resolves to dbg(),
+	 * which is syslog(), which writes through the serial driver. As soon
+	 * as the transmit buffer fills, uart_putxmitchar() enables the
+	 * transmit interrupt and waits on dev->xmitsem for it. That interrupt
+	 * is masked, so nothing ever posts the semaphore: the failure is never
+	 * reported and the allocating thread never returns from its malloc.
+	 *
+	 * So claim abort mode for the duration of the dump whatever the
+	 * configuration, and give it back if we are going to return.
+	 */
+	abort_mode = true;
+
 	mfdbg("Allocation failed from %s heap.\n", (heap_type == KERNEL_HEAP) ? KERNEL_STR : USER_STR);
 	mfdbg(" - requested size %u\n", size);
 	if (align) {
@@ -188,6 +206,14 @@ void mm_manage_alloc_fail_dump(struct mm_heap_s *heap, int startidx, int endidx,
 	 */
 	up_cpu_resume_all();
 #endif
+
+	/* Only when we are going to return. With CONFIG_MM_ASSERT_ON_FAIL the
+	 * caller panics next and abort mode is what the crash dump wants, so
+	 * that configuration behaves exactly as it did before.
+	 */
+#ifndef CONFIG_MM_ASSERT_ON_FAIL
+	abort_mode = saved_abort_mode;
+#endif
 }
 
 void mm_manage_alloc_fail(struct mm_heap_s *heap, int startidx, int endidx, size_t size, size_t align, int heap_type, mmaddress_t caller)
-- 
2.43.0

